WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.682
VerifyBeforePay is a working ForgeHacks prototype for the AI and Cybersecurity track.

00:00:04.682 --> 00:00:11.314
It is for someone facing a message that asks for money, a password, or a one-time code.

00:00:11.314 --> 00:00:14.826
The goal is not to declare the sender genuine.

00:00:14.826 --> 00:00:21.849
It is to make the request easier to inspect, then direct the user to a contact obtained independently.

00:00:21.849 --> 00:00:25.360
This edited walkthrough uses fictional messages and synthetic narration.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:28.937
Here is the built-in fictional example.

00:00:28.937 --> 00:00:34.484
The message claims to come from a bank and asks for a one-time code.

00:00:34.484 --> 00:00:38.050
The expected domain is bank dot example, supplied separately.

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:42.804
The pasted link includes that familiar-looking name, but also an at sign.

00:00:42.804 --> 00:00:44.389
We press Examine message.

00:00:44.389 --> 00:00:51.124
The app does not open the link, send a payment, access an account, or contact the organization.

00:00:52.324 --> 00:00:54.588
The analysis keeps sender identity unverified.

00:00:54.588 --> 00:01:03.269
A small learned model returns an elevated old SMS spam signal, with a score of zero point nine eight nine for this example.

00:01:03.269 --> 00:01:06.288
That number is not a probability of fraud.

00:01:06.288 --> 00:01:09.684
Separate checks identify the actual words urgent and OTP.

00:01:09.684 --> 00:01:16.100
They show observed language, not a conclusion about the sender or the meaning of the whole message.

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:20.447
The actual link host is fraud dot example.

00:01:20.447 --> 00:01:25.560
The bank-looking text before the at sign is user information, not the host.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:31.067
The app shows both that structure and the mismatch with the independently supplied domain.

00:01:31.067 --> 00:01:41.294
It then gives three actions: pause payment or disclosure, use a separately obtained contact or official app, and confirm the request through that channel before acting.

00:01:42.494 --> 00:01:47.830
Now we replace the message with a fictional request to meet after class.

00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:50.294
Its spam signal is not elevated.

00:01:50.294 --> 00:01:56.451
Sender identity still remains unverified, and the same limits and independent verification steps remain visible.

00:01:56.451 --> 00:02:00.146
No listed wording cues is not a safety certificate.

00:02:00.146 --> 00:02:03.840
The model can miss new scams and unsupported languages.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:08.766
Even a matching domain or HTTPS cannot prove who sent a message.

00:02:09.966 --> 00:02:14.889
Clear removes the message, expected domain, and displayed result from the interface.

00:02:14.889 --> 00:02:17.761
It is not a secure memory wipe.

00:02:17.761 --> 00:02:23.915
The browser version downloads static app files, a fixed model, and a self-hosted Python runtime.

00:02:23.915 --> 00:02:27.607
The original Python analysis then runs in browser memory.

00:02:27.607 --> 00:02:31.299
Message text is not uploaded to an inference service.

00:02:31.299 --> 00:02:37.043
The app uses no analytics or message storage, and never learns from pasted messages.

00:02:38.243 --> 00:02:41.974
The public repository explains the model and its limits.

00:02:41.974 --> 00:02:47.779
One frozen, duplicate-grouped evaluation used one thousand and twenty-three older English SMS test groups.

00:02:47.779 --> 00:02:56.486
Spam F one was zero point nine four eight, compared with zero point five six six for a fixed keyword baseline.

00:02:56.486 --> 00:02:58.974
These are not modern fraud results.

00:02:58.974 --> 00:03:03.949
Thirty-four synthetic software checks also matched native Python with the browser runtime.

00:03:03.949 --> 00:03:05.607
No model was retuned.

00:03:05.607 --> 00:03:10.583
Source, a browser demo, and this video are available without an account.

00:03:10.583 --> 00:03:17.631
The intended benefit is a clearer pause and independent check; actual user outcomes have not been measured.

